extends base

block content
    main.main
        section.section-header.section-header--small
            .heading-box
                span.heading-box__eyebrow About Natours
                h1.heading-primary A learning project, built end-to-end

        section.section-description
            .description-box
                h2.heading-secondary.ma-bt-lg The story behind this site

                p.description__text
                    | Natours started as my first attempt at building a complete, real
                    | full-stack web application — front to back, no shortcuts.
                    | I wanted to actually understand how a production-style site works,
                    | not just follow tutorials in pieces.

                p.description__text
                    | It's built with Node.js and Express on the backend, MongoDB as the
                    | database, and Pug for server-side rendering. Along the way I worked
                    | through authentication, file uploads, payments, security, and
                    | everything in between — a lot of it for the first time.

                p.description__text
                    | This wasn't a copy-paste project. Every feature here — from booking
                    | tours to managing reviews to the admin dashboard — was something I
                    | had to figure out, break, and fix myself.

                h2.heading-secondary.ma-bt-lg.ma-bt-sm Why I built it

                p.description__text
                    | I wanted a project big enough to force me to learn things properly:
                    | how a real app handles users, data, and edge cases — not just a
                    | small demo. Natours is that project, and it's still evolving as I
                    | keep learning.

                h2.heading-secondary.ma-bt-lg.ma-bt-sm A quick note

                p.description__text
                    | This is a learning project. I'm proud of how far it's come, and I'm
                    | still actively improving it — so if something looks unfinished
                    | here and there, that's honest work in progress, not neglect.